# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/fixtures_2.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event mabel_closing_up
# en: Turning the Sign
title [8a09ce] = 翻牌打烊
# en: {seen(mabel_closing_up) > 1: {~Closing time again, and Mabel hands you the broom before she's turned the sign. "Staff, near enough," she says.|The square's emptying, and Mabel is putting the chairs up one-handed with a cup of tea in the other. She nods you at the broom.|Mabel has the sign turned before you've finished your cup, and the broom leaning against your chair.}|It's nearly closing at Mabel's, and you're the last one in. She turns the sign without asking you to go. "Chairs up, petal," she says, "if you're stopping."}
text [a367fd] = {seen(mabel_closing_up) > 1: {~又到打烊时分，梅布尔还没翻牌子，就把扫帚递给了你。“差不多算店里的人了，”她说。|广场上的人渐渐散了，梅布尔一手端着茶，一手把椅子倒扣上桌。她朝扫帚努努嘴。|你的茶还没喝完，梅布尔已经翻好了牌子，扫帚就靠在你的椅子边。}|梅布尔茶馆快打烊了，店里只剩你一个客人。她翻过门牌，却没开口请你走。“椅子倒扣上去，小可爱，”她说，“要是你不急着走的话。”}
# en: A small tea shop at closing time, chairs upturned on tables, a woman in an apron counting cups on hooks while a student holds a broom.
scene [7dd8c2] = 打烊时分的小茶馆，椅子倒扣在桌上，一个系围裙的女人数着挂钩上的茶杯，一个学生拿着扫帚。
# en: Put the chairs up and sweep
opt.left.label [441733] = 把椅子倒扣上桌，扫地
# en: {~You sweep while she counts the cups on the hooks, aloud, by name. Some of the names she says more quietly. She sends you home with a heel of cake wrapped in a napkin.|Chairs up, floor swept, the range banked. Mabel tells you who sat where today and what they were really worried about. You promise yourself you'll never tell anyone anything in here.|You put the last chair up and she pours two cups from the bottom of the pot, strong enough to stand a spoon in. "Never waste the last of it," she says. "That's the good part."}
opt.left.say.1 [d6e884] = {~你扫地，她数着挂钩上的杯子，一只一只念出主人的名字。有些名字她念得轻一些。临走她塞给你一块用餐巾包着的蛋糕边。|椅子扣好，地扫净，炉火封上。梅布尔告诉你今天谁坐在哪儿、心里其实在愁什么。你暗暗发誓，在这儿永远什么都不跟任何人说。|你扣上最后一把椅子，她把壶底剩的倒了两杯，浓得能立住勺子。“最后这点千万别浪费，”她说，“这才是好东西。”}
# en: Thank her and catch the barge
opt.right.label [8f2d9c] = 谢过她，赶驳船去
# en: {~"Go on, then," she says, and flaps her cloth at you. You make the barge with a minute to spare.|You leave her to it. Through the window, as the barge pulls out, you see her sit down with the last cup, alone, and look content.|"Mind the step," she says, as she always does. You do, for once.}
opt.right.say.1 [c38c63] = {~“去吧去吧，”她说，朝你甩甩抹布。你赶上驳船时还剩一分钟。|你让她自己收拾。驳船开动时，你从窗外看见她坐下来，独自端着最后一杯茶，一脸满足。|“当心台阶，”她说，每次都这么说。这一回你真的当心了。}

@event mabel_wrong_pot
# en: The Wrong Pot
title [2e5862] = 上错的茶
# en: Mabel brings you a pot of the calm blend before you've sat down, as she always does. But you came in wanting ginger, the courage one, and for once she didn't see it. She looks at the pot, then at you, and her face goes very still. "Well," she says. "That's never happened."
text [f8cb8c] = 你还没坐下，梅布尔就端来一壶安神茶，一向如此。可你今天进门想要的是姜茶，壮胆的那种，而她头一回没看出来。她看看茶壶，又看看你，脸色一下子僵住了。“哎呀，”她说，“这可从来没有过。”
# en: A tea shop counter, a woman in an apron frowning at a teapot as if it had betrayed her, a student holding a cup that isn't what they wanted.
scene [e5c14f] = 茶馆柜台前，系围裙的女人皱眉盯着茶壶，仿佛被它背叛了，一个学生端着一杯不是自己想要的茶。
# en: Tell her she got it wrong
opt.left.label [b18dff] = 告诉她上错了
# en: She laughs, a little too long, and goes to make the ginger. When she brings it she sits down across from you. "You've changed, petal," she says. "I was reading last year's you." She doesn't charge for either pot, and she looks at you differently for the rest of the afternoon.
opt.left.say.1 [4a5cbf] = 她笑了，笑得有点久，转身去泡姜茶。端来时，她在你对面坐下。“你变了，小可爱，”她说，“我读的还是去年的你。”两壶茶她都没收钱，那天下午她看你的眼神一直不太一样。
# en: Drink it and say it's perfect
opt.right.label [3b630a] = 喝下去，说正合适
# en: It's a good pot, and it does calm you. Mabel watches you drink it with her head on one side. She doesn't believe you, quite, and she doesn't say so. When you leave she's still frowning at the tin.
opt.right.say.1 [350011] = 这壶茶不错，也确实让你平静下来。梅布尔歪着头看你喝。她不太信你，但也没说破。你离开时，她还在对着茶罐皱眉。

@event mabel_empty_hook
# en: An Empty Hook
title [5f709a] = 空着的挂钩
# en: Mabel stops you in the square. One of her regulars hasn't been in for a week: an old man who has had the same pot at the same table every afternoon for thirty years. His cup is still on its hook. "His cottage is up the hill past the mill," she says. "I can't leave the shop, petal. Would you look in on him?"
text [861a00] = 梅布尔在广场上拦住你。她的一位老主顾已经一个星期没来了：一个老头，三十年来每天下午坐同一张桌、喝同一壶茶。他的杯子还挂在钩上。“他的小屋在磨坊再往上的山坡上，”她说，“我走不开，小可爱。你能去看看他吗？”
# en: A row of cups on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, one hook empty, a woman in an apron looking at it instead of at the customers.
scene [353071] = 茶馆柜台后一排挂钩上挂着茶杯，有一个钩子空着，系围裙的女人盯着它，没在看客人。
# en: Walk up the hill and look in on him
opt.left.label [ec3961] = 上山去看看他
# en: He's in bed with a cold and a bad temper, and very pleased to be found. You light his fire, fetch his post and stay for an hour of complaints about the weather. When you tell Mabel, she takes down his cup and fills it, and sends you back up the hill with it, wrapped in a cloth.
opt.left.say.1 [326db2] = 他感冒躺在床上，脾气很坏，被人找到却很高兴。你替他生了火、取了信，又听他抱怨了一个钟头的天气。你回去告诉梅布尔，她取下他的杯子斟满，用布包好，让你再送上山去。
# en: Say you've a lecture to get to
opt.right.label [42470a] = 说你还得去上课
# en: "Of course," says Mabel, too quickly. You make your lecture and take good notes. That evening, a barge hand tells you she shut the shop at three and walked up the hill herself. The next week the old man is back at his table, and Mabel is a little short with you over the change.
opt.right.say.1 [87db03] = “当然，”梅布尔说，说得太快了。你赶上了课，笔记记得很好。那天晚上，一个驳船伙计告诉你，她三点就关了店，自己走上了山。第二个星期，那老头又坐回了他的桌子，梅布尔找你零钱时有点冷淡。

@event grimsby_lighthouse_walk
# en: Gone to Look at One
title [ecba87] = 去看灯塔了
# en: Grimsby's door is locked and the sign says BACK SOON (ISH). You know where he goes. Past the jetty the path runs out along the shore to the point, where the old harbour light stands, and there's a small figure in a long coat at the bottom of it, looking up.
text [3fd50a] = 格林斯比书店的门锁着，牌子上写着：马上回来（大概）。你知道他去了哪儿。过了码头，小路沿着湖岸一直伸到岬角，旧港的灯塔就立在那儿，塔脚下站着一个穿长外套的小小身影，仰着头。
# en: A shut bookshop door with a handwritten sign, and beyond the village a long path out to a point where a small lighthouse stands against the sky.
scene [becb03] = 一扇关着的书店门上挂着手写的牌子，村子外一条长长的小路通向岬角，一座小灯塔衬着天空。
# en: Walk out to the point
opt.left.label [67b690] = 走到岬角去
# en: He isn't surprised to see you. "Twelve-sided," he says, pointing. "The lamp room. Most are round." For an hour he tells you how the light was carried up in pieces in 1702, and who kept it, and why it went out. You don't buy a single book that day, and you walk back knowing more than you did.
opt.left.say.1 [a5cb3d] = 他见到你并不意外。“十二边形，”他指着说，“灯室。大多数是圆的。”整整一个钟头，他给你讲这盏灯如何在1702年被一块块扛上去，谁守过它，又为什么熄了。那天你一本书也没买，回去时却比来时多知道了不少。
# en: Come back another day
opt.right.label [657555] = 改天再来
# en: You leave him to his lighthouse. On the barge home you can still see him at the foot of it, small and very still, with his hat in his hand.
opt.right.say.1 [c88835] = 你让他和他的灯塔待着。坐驳船回去时，你还能看见他站在塔脚下，小小的，一动不动，帽子拿在手里。

@event grimsby_read_aloud
# en: The Small Print
title [3e318c] = 小字
# en: {seen(grimsby_read_aloud) > 1: {~Grimsby has a book ready when you come in, and the chair pulled out. "This one smells of rain," he says. "Read me how it starts."|"My eyes," says Mr Grimsby, with dignity, "are on holiday." He hands you a book about the sea and settles back with his eyes closed.|Grimsby holds up a book and pulls out the chair in one movement. "Ten pages," he says. "I'll know by ten pages."}|Mr Grimsby is holding a book at the full length of his arm and squinting. "The print's got smaller," he says, "since 1960. Would you? Just the first chapter. I want to know if it smells as good as it reads."}
text [cf5dc5] = {seen(grimsby_read_aloud) > 1: {~你一进门，格林斯比已经备好了书，椅子也拉开了。“这本闻着有雨的味道，”他说，“给我念念开头。”|“我的眼睛，”格林斯比先生郑重地说，“休假去了。”他递给你一本讲大海的书，往后一靠，闭上了眼。|格林斯比一手举起书，一手拉开椅子。“十页，”他说，“念到十页我就知道了。”}|格林斯比先生把一本书举到手臂伸直的地方，眯着眼看。“这字，”他说，“从1960年起就越印越小了。你能念念吗？就第一章。我想知道它读起来是不是跟闻起来一样好。”}
# en: A cluttered bookshop at dusk, an old man in half-moon glasses holding a book at arm's length, a student in the chair opposite.
scene [50f591] = 黄昏时杂乱的书店，戴半月形眼镜的老人把书举得老远，一个学生坐在他对面的椅子上。
# en: Read to him
opt.left.label [799204] = 念给他听
# en: {~You read until the lamp needs lighting, and your own reading waits. He stops you twice to argue with the author and once to fall asleep. When he wakes he lends you the book "until you've finished it for both of us".|Two chapters, a pot of tea and a long argument about whether the keeper should have married the cook. Your own reading waits another night.|He listens with his eyes shut and his fingers moving, as if turning pages. The evening you'd meant for your essay is gone. At the end he says, "Yes. That one smells right," and gives you the next one.}
opt.left.say.1 [21f6ac] = {~你一直念到该点灯，自己的功课只好先放着。他两次打断你跟作者争论，一次睡着了。醒来后他把书借给你，“等你替我们俩读完再还”。|两章书、一壶茶，外加一场漫长的争论：守灯人到底该不该娶那个厨娘。你自己的书又得再等一晚。|他闭着眼听，手指微微动着，像在翻页。你原本留给论文的那个晚上没了。念完时他说：“对。这本闻着就对了。”然后把下一本递给你。}
# en: Say you've your own reading
opt.right.label [6a9c7c] = 说你自己还有书要读
# en: {~"Of course," says Grimsby, a little lost. He reads the first page himself, very slowly, holding it almost at the window. You go back to your own books.|He nods and puts the book back on the shelf, a little slowly. Then he hands you one for your essay instead, the wrong one, which turns out to have exactly the chapter you needed.|"Next week, then," he says, as if it were a promise. You get your own reading done, and think about him squinting at the window.}
opt.right.say.1 [51e94d] = {~“当然，”格林斯比说，有点茫然。他自己念起第一页，念得很慢，书几乎贴到了窗前。你回去读自己的书。|他点点头，把那本书放回书架，动作有点慢。然后他递给你一本给论文用的书，拿错了，结果里面恰好有你要的那一章。|“那就下个星期，”他说，像是一个约定。你把自己的书读完了，心里却想着他眯着眼凑在窗前的样子。}

@event marisol_exam_calm
# en: Holding the Lake Still
title [697d78] = 让湖静下来
# en: {seen(marisol_exam_calm) > 1: {~Exams again, and Marisol at your window before dawn with the lantern. "The lake remembers you," she says. "Come and hold it."|The lantern is waiting on the jetty with your name chalked on it. Marisol is already in the water, arms out.|"It's in a mood again," says Marisol at the jetty, nodding at the grey water. She hands you the lantern without asking.}|Exams are coming, and Professor Marisol is out on the jetty before dawn. "Somebody always fails because the lake's in a mood," she says. "Not this year. Hold the lantern where the water can see it, and don't talk. The lake listens to everything."}
text [25122d] = {seen(marisol_exam_calm) > 1: {~又到考试，天没亮玛丽索尔就提着灯笼来敲你的窗。“湖还记得你，”她说，“来，拿着。”|灯笼已经在码头上等你了，上面用粉笔写着你的名字。玛丽索尔已经站在水里，张开双臂。|“它又闹脾气了，”玛丽索尔在码头上说，朝灰色的湖水点点头。她一句话不问，就把灯笼递给了你。}|快考试了，玛丽索尔教授天没亮就站在码头上。“每年总有人因为湖闹脾气考砸，”她说，“今年不行。把灯笼举在湖水看得见的地方，别说话。湖什么都听得见。”}
# en: A grey dawn on a lake, a professor waist-deep with her arms out over the water, a student on the jetty with a lantern.
scene [90843e] = 灰蒙蒙的黎明湖面，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里，双臂伸向水面，一个学生提着灯笼站在码头上。
# en: Hold the lantern up for her
opt.left.label [240218] = 替她把灯笼举高
# en: {~An hour in the cold, the lantern up, your arms shaking. The waves go long and slow and then stop, like a sleeper turning over. Marisol whoops once, very quietly, as if the lake might mind.|You hold the light until you can't feel your fingers. Under it the water calms in rings, the way a cat settles. "It likes you," says Marisol. "Or it likes the lantern. Either will do."|The lake fights her for a while and then gives in all at once, flat as a plate to the far shore. Marisol is laughing. You are blue.}
opt.left.say.1 [a7b2a4] = {~在寒风里站了一个钟头，灯笼举着，胳膊直发抖。浪变得又长又缓，然后停了，像熟睡的人翻了个身。玛丽索尔欢呼了一声，很轻很轻，仿佛怕湖介意。|你举着灯，直到手指没了知觉。灯下的湖水一圈圈平静下来，像猫趴下去那样。“它喜欢你，”玛丽索尔说，“要么是喜欢这灯笼。哪个都行。”|湖跟她较了好一阵劲，然后一下子服了软，平得像一只盘子，一直平到对岸。玛丽索尔在笑。你冻得发紫。}
# en: Plead revision and go back to bed
opt.right.label [ae51dc] = 推说要复习，回去睡觉
# en: {~"Of course," she says. "Somebody should pass." You hear her out there, through your window, singing to the water until the bell.|You go back to your notes. The lake on exam day is perfectly calm anyway, and you feel, for some reason, slightly left out.|She waves you off with a wet hand. "Go and learn something," she calls. "The lake and I will manage."}
opt.right.say.1 [429345] = {~“当然，”她说，“总得有人考过。”隔着窗，你听见她在外面对着湖水唱歌，一直唱到钟响。|你回去看笔记。考试那天湖本来就平平静静，不知为什么，你却觉得自己被落下了。|她挥着湿漉漉的手打发你走。“去学点东西吧，”她喊道，“湖和我应付得来。”}

@event marisol_lost_satchel
# en: Ask, Don't Fetch
title [79463c] = 要请，别捞
# en: {seen(marisol_lost_satchel) > 1: {~Another satchel in the lake, another first-year in tears on the jetty. Marisol is wading out, shaking her head. "Ask it," she says to you. "You know how."|A boy's whole term of notes is under the jetty. Marisol hands you the job without a word, just a nod at the water.|Marisol is already waving you down to the jetty, where a third-year's satchel is sinking slowly into the reeds. "Yours," she says. "Ask nicely."}|A first-year has dropped her satchel off the jetty: all her notes for the term. Professor Marisol, already in the water, says, "Don't you dare dive. The lake gives back what it's asked for nicely." She looks at you. "Go on. Ask."}
text [aa9667] = {seen(marisol_lost_satchel) > 1: {~又一个书包掉进湖里，又一个一年级生在码头上哭。玛丽索尔正往水里走，直摇头。“请它还，”她对你说，“你会的。”|一个男孩整学期的笔记都沉在码头底下。玛丽索尔一句话没说，只朝水面点点头，就把这活儿交给了你。|玛丽索尔已经在招手叫你下码头了，一个三年级生的书包正慢慢沉进芦苇里。“归你了，”她说，“客气点请。”}|一个一年级女生把书包掉下了码头：她这学期的全部笔记。玛丽索尔教授已经站在水里，说：“不准跳下去。湖会把东西还给好好请它的人。”她看着你。“去吧。请它。”}
# en: A lakeshore, a first-year crying on the jetty, a professor up to her knees in the water, a satchel's strap just visible far below the surface.
scene [e0eac8] = 湖岸边，一个一年级生在码头上哭，一位教授站在没膝的水里，深深的水底隐约露出一截书包带。
# en: Ask the lake for it
opt.left.label [698904] = 请湖把它还回来
# en: {~You ask, politely, the way she taught you. The water bulges and the satchel comes up into your hands, the notes inside barely damp. Marisol whoops. The first-year looks at you as if you'd walked on the lake.|The lake considers your request for a long, cold moment, then hands the satchel up as if it had been holding it for you. Marisol says, "Thank you," to the water, and makes you say it too.|It comes up slowly, turning, and every page inside is dry. "That," says Marisol, "is how you ask."}
opt.left.say.1 [8f93d7] = {~你照她教的那样，客客气气地请。湖水鼓起来，书包浮到你手里，里面的笔记几乎没湿。玛丽索尔欢呼起来。那个一年级生看你的眼神，就像你刚从湖面上走过来。|湖把你的请求掂量了冷冰冰的好一会儿，然后把书包递了上来，像是一直替你收着。玛丽索尔对着湖水说了声“谢谢”，还让你也说一遍。|它慢慢地、打着转浮上来，里面每一页都是干的。“这个，”玛丽索尔说，“才叫请。”}
# en: {~The lake gives you back a boot, a bottle and a very old fish. Marisol fetches the satchel herself, in two words. "It doesn't know you yet," she says, kindly.|The water rises, sulks and falls. Marisol takes over. "Too much demand in it," she says. "You'll get there."|The lake sends up the satchel's strap and keeps the satchel. Marisol sighs and gets it in one gesture. "Ask," she says. "Don't fetch."}
opt.left.say.2 [edba2d] = {~湖还给你一只靴子、一个瓶子和一条很老的鱼。玛丽索尔自己把书包取了上来，只用了两个词。“它还不认识你，”她温和地说。|湖水涨起来，赌了会儿气，又落了下去。玛丽索尔接了手。“命令的味道太重了，”她说，“你会学会的。”|湖送上来书包带，书包却留下了。玛丽索尔叹口气，一个手势就取了上来。“要请，”她说，“别捞。”}
# en: Leave it to her and go revise
opt.right.label [3faf86] = 交给她，自己回去复习
# en: {~Marisol gets it in one gesture and a whispered word. You hear about it at supper; you were in the library by then, your own notes open.|You don't stay to watch. By the time Marisol has asked the lake, you're at your desk with your own notes dry.|You leave her to it and go back to your revision. She mentions it later, mildly: "You'd have got it, you know."}
opt.right.say.1 [613ded] = {~玛丽索尔一个手势、一句耳语就把它取了上来。晚饭时你才听说这事；那时你已经在图书馆里摊开了自己的笔记。|你没留下来看。等玛丽索尔请完湖，你已经坐在书桌前，自己的笔记干干爽爽。|你让她去处理，自己回去复习。后来她轻描淡写地提了一句：“你本来能行的，你知道吧。”}

@event vey_lunch_dare
# en: Fool Me Once
title [578dd2] = 骗我一次
# en: {seen(vey_lunch_dare) > 1: {~Vey sits down across your lunch with the same smile as last time. "Shall we?" she says.|"Pudding's in five minutes," says Professor Vey, sitting down. "That's plenty of time to deceive a professor."|Vey drops into the seat opposite with her chin on her hands. "The pudding," she says, "is a custard tart. Probably. Fool me."}|Professor Vey sits down opposite you at lunch, which nobody does. "One trick," she says. "Any trick. Fool me before the pudding and I'll owe you one." She folds her hands and watches you with enormous interest.}
text [73e530] = {seen(vey_lunch_dare) > 1: {~维伊在你的午饭对面坐下，笑容和上次一模一样。“来吧？”她说。|“五分钟后上布丁，”维伊教授坐下来说，“骗一位教授，时间绰绰有余。”|维伊往对面座位上一坐，双手托着下巴。“今天的布丁，”她说，“是蛋挞。大概吧。骗我。”}|维伊教授在午饭时坐到你对面，这是谁都不会做的事。“一个把戏，”她说，“什么把戏都行。布丁上来之前骗过我，我就欠你一次。”她十指交叉，饶有兴致地盯着你。}
# en: A long hall at lunch, a professor in a theatrical scarf leaning across a table towards a student, a salt cellar between them.
scene [2e2c62] = 午饭时的长厅，一位围着夸张围巾的教授隔着桌子探向一个学生，两人之间放着一只盐罐。
# en: Try to fool her
opt.left.label [197e52] = 试着骗过她
# en: {~You glamour the salt cellar into a pepper pot and leave the pepper pot alone. She picks up the wrong one, tastes it and laughs out loud, and the whole table turns. "Owed," she says. "Owed."|You don't do any magic at all; you only say that you have. She spends the pudding checking everything on the table. "Oh," she says, when she realises. "Oh, that's good."|She smells the trick before she sees it, and you'd planned for that. She claps, once, delighted. "A student who knows about the nose," she says. "Come to my office hours. Bring that."}
opt.left.say.1 [d1f66b] = {~你给盐罐施了魅影，让它看起来像胡椒瓶，却没动那只胡椒瓶。她拿错了，尝了一口，放声大笑，满桌人都转过头来。“欠你的，”她说，“欠你的。”|你根本没施法，只是说你施了。她整顿布丁时间都在检查桌上的每样东西。“哦，”她回过神来，“哦，这招好。”|她还没看见把戏就先闻出来了，而你早料到了这一点。她拍了一下手，乐坏了。“一个懂得鼻子的学生，”她说，“来我的答疑时间。把这招带上。”}
# en: {~She sniffs, smiles and taps the real salt cellar without looking. "Lavender," she says. "Your glamours always smell of lavender." She seems to like you more for trying, which stings a little.|"Nearly," she says, which is worse than "no". She takes your pudding as forfeit, and eats it slowly, in front of you.|She sees it at once and tells you why, in detail, over the pudding, with the whole table listening. Useful, and humiliating.}
opt.left.say.2 [dc6228] = {~她嗅了嗅，笑了，看都不看就点了点真正的盐罐。“薰衣草，”她说，“你的魅影总有股薰衣草味。”她似乎因为你肯试而更喜欢你了，这反倒有点扎心。|“差一点，”她说，这比“不行”还难受。她把你的布丁当作罚没，当着你的面慢慢吃完。|她一眼就看穿了，还在吃布丁时当着满桌人把原因一五一十讲给你听。很有用，也很丢脸。}
# en: Plead an empty head and eat
opt.right.label [1f938e] = 说脑子空空，埋头吃饭
# en: {~"A sensible answer," says Vey, "and a boring one." She steals a roll off your plate as she goes, or seems to. Later you find it still there.|She sighs theatrically and moves on to the next table, where a second-year is already sweating.|"Another time," she says. "I'll know when you're ready. You'll smell of lavender."}
opt.right.say.1 [e62ca3] = {~“明智的回答，”维伊说，“也很无聊。”她走时从你盘里顺走一个面包卷，或者看起来是这样。后来你发现它还在。|她夸张地叹口气，转到下一桌去了，那儿一个二年级生已经开始冒汗。|“下次吧，”她说，“你准备好了我会知道的。你身上会有薰衣草味。”}

@event vey_rehearsal
# en: An Audience of Nobody
title [c47c6c] = 空无一人的听众
# en: {seen(vey_rehearsal) > 1: {~Vey's illusory students are back, all thirty of them, nodding at everything. "They agree with me," she says, disgusted. "Disagree with me. Loudly."|The ghostly class again. Vey points you to the back row. "Be the worst student I've ever had," she says. "You'll find it easy."|Vey's theatre is full of not-quite students once more. "Same rules," she says. "Ruin it."}|Through an open door in the lecture tower you see Professor Vey lecturing to a full theatre. Then you notice the students are all a little transparent at the edges. "Rehearsing," she says, without turning round. "They're too polite. Come and heckle."}
text [06e9f4] = {seen(vey_rehearsal) > 1: {~维伊的幻影学生又来了，整整三十个，什么都点头。“他们都同意我，”她嫌弃地说，“反对我。大声点。”|又是那个幽灵班级。维伊指指后排。“当我教过的最差的学生，”她说，“你会觉得很容易。”|维伊的讲堂里又坐满了不太真实的学生。“老规矩，”她说，“搅黄它。”}|透过讲堂塔里一扇开着的门，你看见维伊教授正对着满满一堂人讲课。接着你注意到，那些学生的边缘都有点透明。“在排练，”她头也不回地说，“他们太客气了。来起个哄。”}
# en: A lecture theatre in the afternoon, rows of glamoured students who flicker at the edges, a professor at the lectern, one real student in the doorway.
scene [a52050] = 午后的讲堂，一排排施了魅影的学生边缘闪烁，教授站在讲台前，唯一真实的学生站在门口。
# en: Take the back row and heckle
opt.left.label [25d2e5] = 坐到后排起哄
# en: {~You ask the question everyone wants to ask and nobody does. Vey stops, thinks, and rewrites half her lecture on the spot, and the afternoon you'd meant for your reading goes with it. The illusory class applauds you, which is unnerving.|You heckle so well that one of the illusions turns round and glares at you. Vey is enchanted, and keeps you two hours. "Thursday," she says. "Same time. Bring worse questions."|An hour of interruptions and one genuinely good objection. "That one stays in," says Vey, writing it down. You leave with a sore throat and less of your own reading done.}
opt.left.say.1 [1ff0b1] = {~你问了一个人人都想问、却没人开口的问题。维伊停下来想了想，当场改写了半堂课，你原本留给读书的下午也跟着没了。幻影班级为你鼓掌，叫人心里发毛。|你起哄起得太好，有个幻影竟转过身来瞪你。维伊高兴极了，留了你两个钟头。“星期四，”她说，“老时间。带更糟的问题来。”|一个钟头的打岔，外加一个真正有分量的反驳。“这个留下，”维伊边说边记下来。你走时嗓子哑了，自己的书也少读了不少。}
# en: Close the door quietly and go
opt.right.label [189140] = 轻轻关上门离开
# en: {~You leave her to her polite ghosts. Through the door, as you go, you hear thirty of them laugh at a joke at exactly the same moment.|You slip away. Behind you, Vey says, "Coward," quite cheerfully, without looking round.|You go. The illusions watch you leave, all of them, in perfect unison.}
opt.right.say.1 [4a4a3f] = {~你让她和那些客气的幽灵待着。走的时候，你听见门里三十个人在同一瞬间为一个笑话笑出声来。|你悄悄溜走。身后维伊头也不回，挺高兴地说了声：“胆小鬼。”|你走了。那些幻影齐刷刷地目送你离开。}

@event brask_half_sentences
# en: Pliers. Not Those.
title [dbb0a4] = 钳子。不是那个。
# en: {~Professor Brask has a hippogriff's wing pinned open with both arms and a splinter of fence rail in its feathers. "Pliers," she says. "Not those. The. Yes. No. Hold the." The hippogriff has opinions about all of it.|A griffin has a thorn deep in its forepaw and Brask has the paw in her lap. "Salve. Then the. Then hold. Not there." She doesn't look round. The tray holds eleven things.|An owl with a bent flight feather, and Brask's hands full of owl. "The splint. The little. Tape. No, the." The owl looks at you as if it, too, would like a whole sentence.}
text [aaa60a] = {~布拉斯克教授用两条胳膊撑开一只鹰头马身有翼兽的翅膀，羽毛里扎着一根围栏木刺。“钳子，”她说，“不是那个。那个。对。不对。按住那个。”这只鹰头马身有翼兽对这一切都有意见。|一头狮鹫前爪深深扎了根刺，布拉斯克把那只爪子搁在腿上。“药膏。然后那个。然后按住。不是那儿。”她没回头。托盘上摆着十一样东西。|一只猫头鹰折弯了一根飞羽，布拉斯克两手抱满了猫头鹰。“夹板。小的那个。胶布。不，那个。”猫头鹰看着你，好像它也想听一句完整的话。}
# en: A stable stall, a professor holding a hippogriff's wing open with both arms, a splinter of fence in the feathers, a student by a tray of tools.
scene [590198] = 马厩隔间里，一位教授用两条胳膊撑开鹰头马身有翼兽的翅膀，羽毛里扎着一根围栏木刺，一个学生站在工具托盘旁。
# en: Work out what she means
opt.left.label [9988c2] = 揣摩她的意思
# en: {~You read her the way she reads them: which hand is free, what she's looking at, what comes next. The splinter comes out clean. Brask nods once, which from her is a speech.|Salve, then the bandage, then you hold the paw where she was looking. It's done in a minute. "Huh," says Brask, and later you hear her tell the griffin about you, in a full sentence.|Splint, tape, the little scissors, in that order, before she's finished asking. The owl stops complaining. "Good," says Brask. "Most people hand me the big scissors."}
opt.left.say.1 [32f1d6] = {~你像她读懂动物那样读懂她：哪只手空着，她在看哪儿，下一步要什么。木刺干净利落地拔了出来。布拉斯克点了一下头，这在她就算是一篇演讲了。|先上药膏，再缠绷带，然后你按住她刚才看着的地方。一分钟就弄完了。“嗯，”布拉斯克说。后来你听见她对那头狮鹫讲起你，用的是完整的句子。|夹板、胶布、小剪刀，按这个顺序，在她说完之前就递了过去。猫头鹰不叫唤了。“好，”布拉斯克说，“大多数人递给我的是大剪刀。”}
# en: {~You hand her the wrong pliers and the hippogriff takes the opportunity to bite you. Brask gets the splinter out one-handed and says, "Moral there."|You hold the paw where she wasn't looking. The griffin explains your mistake with its other paw. Brask finishes the job and binds your hand too, briskly.|The owl gets loose and spends ten minutes on the beams, and so do you. Brask fixes the feather when it comes down, and two words on you, both short.}
opt.left.say.2 [dc0a05] = {~你递错了钳子，那只鹰头马身有翼兽趁机咬了你一口。布拉斯克单手拔出木刺，说：“这里头有个教训。”|你按住的不是她看着的地方。狮鹫用另一只爪子给你讲解了你的错误。布拉斯克干完活，顺手利落地把你的手也包上了。|猫头鹰挣脱了，在房梁上待了十分钟，你也跟着在上面待了十分钟。它下来后布拉斯克修好了羽毛，又对你说了两个字，都很短。}
# en: Ask her to say the whole sentence
opt.right.label [f93bea] = 请她把整句话说完
# en: {~"Hm," says Brask, and tells you, slowly, all of it, with the history of every tool and the creature it was last used on. You never forget it. You are late for everything.|She says the whole sentence, and the next three, and the next thirty, and the splinter comes out while she's talking. Your afternoon goes with it. She doesn't think less of you for asking. She does remember that you asked.|"Oh," she says, surprised. "The splint. Obviously." Then she explains why it's obvious for half an hour.}
opt.right.say.1 [ab3cac] = {~“嗯，”布拉斯克说，然后慢慢地把一切都告诉了你，连每样工具的来历和它上回用在哪只动物身上都讲了。你一辈子忘不了。你干什么都迟到了。|她把整句话说完了，接着又说了三句、三十句，木刺在她说话时就拔出来了。你的下午也跟着没了。她没因为你问而看低你。她确实记住了你问过。|“哦，”她有些意外，“夹板。当然了。”然后她花了半个钟头解释这为什么是当然的。}

@event brask_laugh
# en: A Bite and a Moral
title [fe1663] = 一口咬和一个教训
# en: Professor Brask is leaning on the paddock fence beside you, which she doesn't do. "Heard you tell a story at lunch," she says. "Badly. Stories need a bite at the end. And a moral. Go on, then. One with both."
text [8c4465] = 布拉斯克教授靠在你身边的围场栅栏上，这可不是她会做的事。“午饭时听你讲了个故事，”她说，“讲得很糟。故事结尾得有一口咬。还得有个教训。来吧，讲一个两样都有的。”
# en: A professor in a stained leather coat leaning on a fence by a paddock, a student beside her mid-story, a hippogriff listening.
scene [67fa63] = 一位穿着污渍斑斑皮外套的教授靠在围场栅栏上，身边的学生正讲着故事，一只鹰头马身有翼兽在旁听。
# en: Tell her the one about the goose
opt.left.label [0f22c7] = 讲那个大鹅的故事
# en: {~You tell her about the goose and the porter's hat, with the bite exactly where it should be and the moral last. Brask laughs, once, loudly, the whole term's laugh in one go. The hippogriff startles. "Well," she says, wiping her eyes. "That's the term done."|You get the bite right and the moral wrong, on purpose, and that's the joke. She laughs so suddenly that a pigeon falls off the stable roof. Nobody will believe you later.|It's a short story. The bite is a real one and the moral is "Don't." Brask laughs like a door slamming, then looks round to see who heard.}
opt.left.say.1 [447cc7] = {~你讲了大鹅和门房帽子的故事，那一口咬恰到好处，教训放在最后。布拉斯克笑了，就一声，很响，把一整个学期的笑一次用完了。鹰头马身有翼兽吓了一跳。“好吧，”她擦着眼睛说，“这学期就到这儿了。”|你把那一口咬讲对了，教训却故意讲错，笑点就在这儿。她笑得那么突然，一只鸽子从马厩顶上掉了下来。之后没人会信你。|故事很短。那一口是真咬，教训是“别”。布拉斯克笑得像门被摔上，然后四下张望，看谁听见了。}
# en: {~She listens all the way through and says, "No bite." Then she tells you the same story properly, with a bite, and it is much better, and she doesn't laugh at all.|"Moral's in the wrong place," says Brask. "Should be after the bite. Never before." She tells you three of hers to show you. You're both quite pleased by the end.|She doesn't laugh, but she nods, which is a start. "Practise on the goats," she says. "They're honest."}
opt.left.say.2 [4554f0] = {~她从头听到尾，说：“没有咬。”然后她把同一个故事好好地、带着一口咬重讲了一遍，好听多了，而她自己一下都没笑。|“教训放错了地方，”布拉斯克说，“得放在咬之后。绝不能在前头。”她讲了三个自己的故事给你示范。到最后你们俩都挺高兴。|她没笑，但点了点头，这算是个开始。“拿山羊练练，”她说，“它们老实。”}
# en: Ask for one of hers instead
opt.right.label [e06ab4] = 请她讲一个她的
# en: {~She tells you about a student who fed a dragon a pear in 1971. The bite is at the end. So is the student. The moral is about pears.|Brask tells you the one about the griffin and the bishop. You'll never be able to look at a bishop again.|She tells you a story about the old barn owl that ends with a bite and no moral at all, then walks off before you can ask about it.}
opt.right.say.1 [dfb736] = {~她给你讲了1971年有个学生喂龙吃梨的事。那一口咬在结尾。那个学生也在结尾。教训跟梨有关。|布拉斯克讲了狮鹫和主教的故事。你以后再也没法正眼看主教了。|她讲了一个关于那只老仓鸮的故事，结尾有一口咬，却没有教训，然后不等你问就走开了。}

@event amaranth_tea_break
# en: Five Minutes Sitting Down
title [110d38] = 坐下五分钟
# en: {seen(amaranth_tea_break) > 1: {~Sister Amaranth is sitting down again, shoes off, and there's a second cup on the bed beside her already. "Five minutes," she says.|The infirmary in the evening, and Amaranth on the edge of an empty bed with her tea. She moves her feet to make room without a word.|"You again," says Amaranth, from the empty bed, cup in both hands. "Good. Sit."}|You go past the infirmary in the evening and Sister Amaranth is sitting down. Nobody has ever seen her sit down. She has her shoes off and a cup of tea in both hands, and she looks at you over it. "Five minutes," she says. "Sit. Don't talk about your health."}
text [a21d62] = {seen(amaranth_tea_break) > 1: {~阿玛兰斯修女又坐着了，鞋子脱了，身边床上已经放了第二只杯子。“五分钟，”她说。|傍晚的医务室，阿玛兰斯坐在一张空床边上喝茶。她一言不发地挪了挪脚，给你腾出地方。|“又是你，”阿玛兰斯坐在空床上，双手捧着杯子说，“好。坐。”}|傍晚你路过医务室，看见阿玛兰斯修女坐着。从来没人见过她坐下。她脱了鞋，双手捧着一杯茶，从杯沿上方看着你。“五分钟，”她说，“坐。别谈你的身体。”}
# en: An empty infirmary in the evening, a healer sitting on the edge of a bed with a cup of tea, shoes off, a student in the doorway.
scene [f0c072] = 傍晚空荡荡的医务室，一位治疗师脱了鞋坐在床沿，端着一杯茶，一个学生站在门口。
# en: Sit with her for the five minutes
opt.left.label [cbc247] = 陪她坐这五分钟
# en: {~You sit. She tells you about the first patient she ever lost, in four short sentences, and then about a cat she had as a girl, in a great many, and the evening you'd meant for your notes goes by. "Keep doing that," she says at last, and you're not sure what she means.|Five minutes stretch to forty, and your reading waits. She talks about her mother, who was a healer too and believed no excuse either. When the bell goes she stands, and is Sister Amaranth again, and sends you off with a list of things to eat.|You don't talk about your health. You talk about the weather, and the bread, and a book she read twice, for longer than you had. When you leave, she's asleep sitting up, with the cup still level in her hands. You take it gently and put it on the shelf.}
opt.left.say.1 [6294fd] = {~你坐下了。她用四个短句讲了她失去的第一个病人，又用很多很多句讲了她小时候养的一只猫，你原本留给笔记的晚上就这么过去了。“继续这样，”她最后说，你不太确定她指的是什么。|五分钟拉长成了四十分钟，你的书只好等着。她讲起她母亲，也是治疗师，也从不相信任何借口。钟一响她就站起来，又成了阿玛兰斯修女，打发你走时还给了你一张该吃什么的单子。|你们没谈你的身体。你们谈天气、谈面包、谈一本她读了两遍的书，谈得比你能留的时间还长。你走的时候，她已经坐着睡着了，杯子在手里还端得平平的。你轻轻接过来，放到架子上。}
# en: Leave her to rest
opt.right.label [5a1262] = 让她歇着
# en: {~"Wise," she says, and closes her eyes. You close the door very quietly.|You back out. In the morning, there's a note on your bedside table: "For not talking."|You leave. From the corridor you hear her sigh, a long one, the kind she never lets anyone hear.}
opt.right.say.1 [c7cb12] = {~“明智，”她说，闭上了眼睛。你轻轻带上门。|你退了出去。第二天早上，你床头桌上有张字条：“谢谢你没说话。”|你走了。从走廊里，你听见她叹了口气，很长的一口，是她从不让人听见的那种。}

@event pell_castle_errand
# en: Forty Drops, Up the Hill
title [f8ee02] = 四十滴，送上山
# en: {seen(pell_castle_errand) > 1: {~"Porter again," says Pell, handing you the bottle. "He'll want to argue about the dose. Don't let him."|Pell holds out the porter's bottle without a word, then, grudgingly: "Forty. He'll argue. Don't let him."|"The porter," says Pell, as if it were a disease, and puts the bottle in your hand. "Forty. Twice."}|Old Pell catches you in the square and puts a corked bottle in your hand. "The castle porter's chest," he says. "Forty drops at night, not forty-one. Tell him. Twice. He won't listen the first time."}
text [ea34f0] = {seen(pell_castle_errand) > 1: {~“又是门房，”佩尔说着把瓶子递给你，“他会跟你争剂量。别让他。”|佩尔一言不发地递过门房的药瓶，然后不情不愿地补了一句：“四十。他会争。别让他。”|“门房，”佩尔说，像在说一种病，把瓶子塞进你手里，“四十。说两遍。”}|老佩尔在广场上拦住你，把一个塞了软木塞的瓶子放进你手里。“城堡门房的胸口，”他说，“晚上四十滴，不是四十一。告诉他。说两遍。第一遍他不会听。”}
# en: An apothecary's back door at dusk, an old man pressing a corked bottle into a student's hand, the castle on its hill across the lake.
scene [de5b74] = 黄昏时药铺的后门，一位老人把一个塞着软木塞的瓶子按进学生手里，湖对岸山上就是城堡。
# en: Carry it up and tell him twice
opt.left.label [2de3f8] = 送上去，跟他说两遍
# en: {~It takes the evening, there and back. The porter argues for forty-five drops. You say forty, twice, the way Pell said it. He takes forty, grumbling. Next morning, he's stopped coughing, and Pell, hearing of it, corrects a label in your honour.|Up the hill with the bottle, and your evening's reading left behind. You deliver the bottle and the dose, twice. The porter says, "Pell's sent a trained one, then," and gives you a biscuit from his secret tin.|You give up an evening and count the forty drops out for him yourself, aloud, the way Pell would. The porter is astonished into silence, which hasn't happened in living memory.}
opt.left.say.1 [29c1fb] = {~一来一回，搭进去一个晚上。门房非要四十五滴。你照佩尔的口气说了两遍：四十。他嘟嘟囔囔地吃了四十滴。第二天早上他不咳了；佩尔听说后，为了你改正了一张标签。|你拿着瓶子上山，晚上的书只好留在身后。你把药和剂量都送到了，说了两遍。门房说：“佩尔派来个训练过的啊。”还从他的秘密铁罐里拿了块饼干给你。|你搭进去一个晚上，亲自替他一滴一滴数出四十滴，像佩尔那样大声数。门房惊得说不出话来，这在人们的记忆里还是头一回。}
# en: Say you're going the other way
opt.right.label [4c9e9d] = 说你要往另一个方向去
# en: {~Pell grunts and gives it to one of his daughters, who rolls her eyes and goes. He doesn't mention it again. He doesn't need to.|"Hm," says Pell, which covers a great deal. He sends the bottle up on the barge with a note that says FORTY in large letters.|He nods and turns away. The porter gets his bottle anyway; Pell carries it up himself, at seventy-seven, in the rain.}
opt.right.say.1 [f1b5f4] = {~佩尔哼了一声，把瓶子交给他的一个女儿，她翻了个白眼就去了。他再没提这事。他也不需要提。|“嗯，”佩尔说，这一个字包含了很多意思。他把瓶子放上驳船送上山，附了张字条，上面大大地写着“四十”。|他点点头，转过身去。门房还是拿到了药；是佩尔自己送上去的，七十七岁，冒着雨。}
